What is claimed is: 

         1 . A system comprising:
 circuitry configured to:
 receive, via a communication interface coupled to a packet-switched network, sensor data from a terminal device and preprocess the sensor data to generate structured context feature data; 
 retrieve sequence data and associated statistical pattern data from a storage; 
 construct an input token sequence by concatenating the structured context feature data, a subset of the sequence data, and a generation instruction specifying output constraints; 
 input the input token sequence to a transformer-based language model and execute autoregressive decoding with a sampling parameter to generate a plurality of output token sequences; 
 compute a relevance score for each output token sequence on the basis of token-level probability, a learned preference vector, and the structured context feature data; 
 apply a filtering rule set to remove output token sequences that match a prohibited pattern; 
 transmit a ranked subset of the output token sequences to the terminal device via the packet-switched network; 
 receive, from the terminal device, a selection signal identifying a selected output token sequence; 
 associate the selection signal with the structured context feature data to generate an evaluation record; and 
 update parameters of at least one of the transformer-based language model and a ranking model on the basis of the evaluation record. 



         2 . The system according to  claim 1 , wherein the circuitry is configured to preprocess the sensor data by mapping location coordinates to a location category using a geospatial lookup, determining a time-of-day category by comparing time data to predefined intervals, and computing an environmental noise level category from audio power spectrum data, and to encode the location category, the time-of-day category, and the noise level category as the structured context feature data. 


         3 . The system according to  claim 2 , wherein the circuitry is configured to preprocess the sequence data by tokenizing text into token identifiers using a vocabulary associated with the transformer-based language model, normalizing character encoding, and computing statistical pattern data comprising token frequency counts, n-gram frequency counts, and sentence length distributions. 


         4 . The system according to  claim 1 , wherein the generation instruction specifies at least a maximum token count for each output token sequence, a language constraint, and a formatting constraint, and wherein the input token sequence further comprises a user attribute segment encoding user preference information derived from the evaluation record. 


         5 . The system according to  claim 4 , wherein the sampling parameter comprises at least one of a temperature value, a top-k threshold, and a nucleus sampling threshold, and wherein the circuitry is configured to parse a text output of the transformer-based language model into the plurality of output token sequences and to compute a confidence score for each output token sequence on the basis of token-level log-probabilities. 


         6 . The system according to  claim 1 , wherein the relevance score is computed by a scoring function that combines the token-level probability with a similarity measure between the output token sequence and the learned preference vector, and a context match score between the output token sequence and the structured context feature data, and wherein the circuitry is configured to rank the plurality of output token sequences in descending order of the relevance score. 


         7 . The system according to  claim 6 , wherein the filtering rule set comprises pattern-matching filters that detect prohibited terms and an auxiliary classification model that assigns a safety score to each output token sequence, and wherein the circuitry is configured to remove output token sequences having a safety score below a predetermined threshold. 


         8 . The system according to  claim 1 , wherein the circuitry is configured to fine-tune the transformer-based language model by feeding sequences of token identifiers derived from the sequence data into the transformer-based language model, computing a cross-entropy loss between predicted token distributions and actual next tokens, computing gradients using backpropagation, and updating model parameters using an adaptive optimization algorithm. 


         9 . The system according to  claim 8 , wherein the circuitry is configured to fine-tune a subset of model parameters comprising adapter modules inserted between layers of the transformer-based language model, while keeping remaining model parameters fixed. 


         10 . The system according to  claim 1 , wherein the circuitry is configured to update the learned preference vector by incrementing frequency counts for tokens contained in the selected output token sequence conditioned on the structured context feature data, and to recompute preference scores used by the scoring function for subsequent ranking operations. 


         11 . The system according to  claim 10 , wherein the circuitry is configured to automatically increase an output priority of output token sequences that are frequently selected under similar structured context feature data on the basis of accumulated evaluation records. 


         12 . The system according to  claim 1 , wherein the selection signal is generated by the terminal device using at least one of a gaze detection mechanism that maps gaze coordinates to a display region and registers a selection when a dwell time exceeds a threshold, a contact detection mechanism that identifies a touch event on a display element, and a voice recognition mechanism that maps a recognized utterance to an output token sequence identifier. 


         13 . The system according to  claim 1 , wherein the circuitry is further configured to cause the terminal device to output the selected output token sequence to a communication partner by at least one of converting the selected output token sequence into synthesized audio using a text-to-speech engine and transmitting the synthesized audio to a speaker, and displaying the selected output token sequence on a display of the terminal device. 


         14 . The system according to  claim 1 , wherein the circuitry is configured to encrypt the sequence data, the structured context feature data, and the evaluation record prior to storage, and to perform communication with the terminal device using an encrypted communication protocol. 


         15 . The system according to  claim 1 , wherein the circuitry is further configured to estimate an emotional state of the user using an emotion identification model applied to at least one of audio data, image data, and behavioral interaction data received from the terminal device, and to select a prompt template for the generation instruction on the basis of the estimated emotional state and the structured context feature data. 


         16 . The system according to  claim 15 , wherein the emotion identification model outputs intensity values for a plurality of emotion categories, and the circuitry is configured to compare each intensity value against a corresponding threshold to determine a user emotional condition, and to adjust at least one of the generation instruction and the scoring function on the basis of the user emotional condition. 


         17 . The system according to  claim 1 , wherein the circuitry is configured to generate a layout parameter on the basis of the relevance score and user attribute information, and to transmit the layout parameter to the terminal device for adaptation of a display format of the ranked subset of output token sequences. 


         18 . A system comprising:
 circuitry configured to:
 receive, via a communication interface coupled to a packet-switched network, sensor data comprising location coordinates, time data, and environmental audio data from a terminal device; 
 map the location coordinates to a location category, determine a time-of-day category from the time data, and compute a noise level category from audio power spectrum data to generate structured context feature data; 
 retrieve, from a storage, tokenized sequence data and associated statistical pattern data comprising token frequency counts and n-gram frequency counts; 
 construct an input token sequence by concatenating the structured context feature data, a subset of the tokenized sequence data, user preference information derived from stored evaluation records, and a generation instruction specifying a maximum token count, a language constraint, and a formatting constraint; 
 input the input token sequence to a transformer-based language model comprising multiple layers of self-attention and feed-forward sublayers, and execute autoregressive decoding with at least one of a temperature value, a top-k threshold, and a nucleus sampling threshold to generate a plurality of output token sequences; 
 compute a relevance score for each output token sequence by combining token-level log-probability, a similarity measure against a learned preference vector, and a context match score; 
 apply a filtering rule set comprising pattern-matching filters and an auxiliary classification model to remove output token sequences having a safety score below a predetermined threshold; 
 transmit a ranked subset of the output token sequences to the terminal device via the packet-switched network; 
 receive, from the terminal device, a selection signal generated by at least one of a gaze detection mechanism, a contact detection mechanism, and a voice recognition mechanism; 
 associate the selection signal with the structured context feature data to generate an evaluation record; 
 update the learned preference vector and at least one of the transformer-based language model and a ranking model on the basis of the evaluation record; and 
 cause the terminal device to output the selected output token sequence to a communication partner via at least one of a text-to-speech engine and a display. 



         19 . The system according to  claim 18 , wherein the circuitry is further configured to estimate an emotional state of a user using an emotion identification model applied to at least one of audio data and image data received from the terminal device, and to adjust at least one of the generation instruction and the scoring function on the basis of the estimated emotional state. 


         20 . A method performed by circuitry of a system, the method comprising:
 receiving, via a communication interface coupled to a packet-switched network, sensor data from a terminal device and preprocessing the sensor data to generate structured context feature data;   retrieving sequence data and associated statistical pattern data from a storage;   constructing an input token sequence by concatenating the structured context feature data, a subset of the sequence data, and a generation instruction specifying output constraints;   inputting the input token sequence to a transformer-based language model and executing autoregressive decoding with a sampling parameter to generate a plurality of output token sequences;   computing a relevance score for each output token sequence on the basis of token-level probability, a learned preference vector, and the structured context feature data;   applying a filtering rule set to remove output token sequences that match a prohibited pattern;   transmitting a ranked subset of the output token sequences to the terminal device via the packet-switched network;   receiving, from the terminal device, a selection signal identifying a selected output token sequence;   associating the selection signal with the structured context feature data to generate an evaluation record; and   updating parameters of at least one of the transformer-based language model and a ranking model on the basis of the evaluation record.

What is claimed is: 

         1 . A computerized system for quantifying relationship dynamics in therapeutic settings, comprising:
 a processor executing instructions stored in non-transitory memory;   a web-based subscription service hosted on a server and accessible to licensed therapists for account registration and client management;   a relational database stored on the server, the database configured to store:
 therapist account records comprising unique therapist identifiers and authentication credentials; 
 client pair records comprising first and second client identifiers linked to a therapist identifier, wherein each client pair represents partners in a therapeutic relationship; and 
 event records comprising sentiment scores, timestamps, and metadata associated with journal entries created by clients in the client pair; 
   a mobile application executing on client mobile devices, the mobile application comprising:
 a user interface configured to receive text-based journal entries describing relationship interactions; 
 a transmission module configured to securely transmit journal entry text to an AI sentiment analysis engine; and 
 a display module configured to present sentiment analysis results to the client; and 
 a local database on the client device for storing text entries, score, reasoning, confidence, neglect, repair, shared and bid indicators, date and time 
   an AI sentiment analysis engine configured to:
 receive journal entry text from the mobile application; 
 process the journal entry text using natural language processing algorithms to identify emotional valence; 
 assign a numerical sentiment score to the journal entry based on a Care-Neglect-Abuse continuum, wherein care interactions receive positive scores ranging from +1 to +10, neglect interactions receive a score of 0, and abuse interactions receive negative scores ranging from −1 to −10; 
 detect repair attempt language patterns within the journal entry text; and 
 apply a positive modifier to the sentiment score when repair attempt language is detected; and 
   a therapist reporting interface accessible via the web-based subscription service, the reporting interface configured to:
 retrieve event records from the relational database for a selected client pair and date range; 
 generate a visual scatter plot displaying sentiment scores plotted against timestamps including color code green for positive scores (Care), otherwise red; 
 overlay a trend line on the scatter plot to illustrate sentiment patterns over time where a positive slope is color coded green, else red. 



         2 . A method for implementing a psychological model for relationship therapy, comprising:
 enrolling a licensed therapist by:
 receiving therapist registration information via a web-based interface; 
 processing subscription payment for annual access to a therapy platform; 
 creating a therapist account record in a relational database with unique authentication credentials; and 
 granting the therapist access to a reporting dashboard; registering a client pair by: 
 receiving identification information for a first client and a second client from the therapist; 
 generating unique login credentials for the first client and the second client; 
 creating client records in the relational database linked to the therapist account record; and 
 providing mobile application installation instructions and login credentials to the first client and the second client; 
   receiving journal entries by:
 accepting text input via a mobile application interface executing on a client mobile device, wherein the text input describes a relationship interaction between the first client and the second client; 
 transmitting the text input to a sentiment analysis engine hosted on a remote server; 
   analyzing sentiment by:
 parsing the text input into semantic units using natural language processing; 
 identifying emotional valence of each semantic unit based on a lexicon of relationship-relevant terms; 
 classifying the text input as representing care, neglect, or abuse based on the identified emotional valence; 
 assigning a numerical score on a scale ranging from −10 to +10, wherein positive scores indicate care, zero indicates neglect, and negative scores indicate abuse; 
 detecting presence of repair attempt language within the text input; 
 applying a +2 modifier to the numerical score when repair attempt language is detected; 
 generating a confidence score indicating certainty of the sentiment classification; and 
 transmitting the numerical score, confidence score, and reasoning explanation back to the mobile application; 
   storing event data by:
 creating an event record in the relational database comprising the numerical score, a timestamp, and metadata identifying the client pair; 
 maintaining the full text of the journal entry locally on the client mobile device without transmitting the full text to the remote server; 
   generating reports by:
 receiving a request from the therapist via the reporting dashboard to view sentiment data for the client pair; 
 querying the relational database for event records associated with the client pair within a specified date range; 
 calculating summary statistics comprising counts of neglect events, repair attempt events, shared entries and bid attempts; 
 generating a scatter plot visualization displaying the numerical scores plotted chronologically and color coded; green for positive scores (Care), otherwise red; 
 overlaying a trend line on the scatter plot using linear regression or moving average calculation with positive slope color coded green, otherwise red; and 
 presenting the scatter plot and summary statistics to the therapist via the reporting dashboard. 



         3 . A non-transitory computer-readable storage medium containing instructions that, when executed by a processor, cause the processor to implement a Care-Neglect-Abuse continuum psychological model by:
 receiving journal entry text via an application programming interface (API),   wherein the journal entry text comprises a narrative description of a relationship interaction;   tokenizing the journal entry text into a plurality of words and phrases;   removing stop words from the plurality of words and phrases to generate a set of semantically significant terms;   comparing the set of semantically significant terms against a sentiment lexicon comprising:
 care-related terms associated with positive numerical values ranging from +1 to +10; 
 neglect-related terms associated with a zero numerical value; and 
 abuse-related terms associated with negative numerical values ranging from −1 to −10; 
   calculating a preliminary sentiment score by aggregating numerical values associated with matching terms from the sentiment lexicon;   analyzing contextual information surrounding the matching terms to adjust the preliminary sentiment score, wherein the contextual information comprises:
 negation words that reverse the polarity of adjacent terms; 
 intensifier words that amplify the magnitude of adjacent terms; and 
 emotion words that indicate subjective emotional states; 
   searching the journal entry text for repair attempt indicators comprising:
 apology language patterns; 
 reconciliation effort phrases; 
 responsibility-taking statements; and 
 soothing behavior expressions; 
   when one or more repair attempt indicators are detected:
 adding a +2 modifier to the preliminary sentiment score to generate a final sentiment score; and 
 setting a repair attempt flag to true; 
   when no repair attempt indicators are detected:
 designating the preliminary sentiment score as the final sentiment score; and 
 setting the repair attempt flag to false; 
   calculating a confidence score ranging from 0.0 to 1.0 based on:
 strength of lexicon matches; 
 clarity of emotional language; and 
 absence of ambiguous or sarcastic phrasing; 
   generating a reasoning explanation describing:
 which terms contributed to the final sentiment score; 
 whether care, neglect, or abuse was detected; and 
 whether a repair attempt was identified; 
   transmitting a response comprising the final sentiment score, the repair attempt flag, the confidence score, and the reasoning explanation via the application programming interface; and   storing the final sentiment score, the repair attempt flag, bid, shared, and neglect flags in a database record associated with a client identifier and a timestamp, wherein the journal entry text is not stored in the database record.   


         4 . The system of  claim 1 , wherein the mobile application further comprises an optional sharing module configured to:
 present a user interface element enabling the client to selectively share a journal entry with their partner via SMS messaging;   upon receiving user input activating the sharing function, transmit the journal entry text to a phone number associated with the partner; and   log the sharing event in the database by creating a sharing record comprising a timestamp, the sentiment score, and identifiers for both the sending client and the receiving partner, wherein the sharing record is accessible to the therapist via the reporting interface.   


         5 . The system of  claim 1 , wherein the AI sentiment analysis engine is further configured to:
 parse the journal entry text into a plurality of sentences;   assign individual sentiment scores to each sentence in the plurality of sentences;   identify a subset of sentences having the highest emotional intensity based on presence of intensifier words, capitalization patterns, or exclamation marks;   apply higher weighting to sentiment scores of the subset of sentences; and   calculate an aggregated sentiment score for the entire journal entry by computing a weighted average of the individual sentiment scores.   


         6 . The system of  claim 1 , wherein the therapist reporting interface is further configured to:
 display repair attempt markers on the scatter plot at data points corresponding to event records with detected repair attempts;   calculate a repair attempt frequency metric indicating a number of repair attempts per week or per month; and   display trend information comparing repair attempt frequency across different time periods.   


         7 . The system of  claim 1 , wherein the relational database further comprises:
 a therapist notes field associated with each event record, the therapist notes field configured to store annotations entered by the therapist via the reporting interface; and   wherein the therapist reporting interface is further configured to:
 display an input field for entering therapist notes when viewing a specific journal entry detail; and 
 save the entered therapist notes to the therapist notes field in the 
 corresponding event record. 



         8 . The method of  claim 2 , further comprising:
 enabling optional partner sharing by:
 receiving a phone number from the first client via the mobile application; 
 storing the phone number in the client record associated with the second client; 
 presenting a share button in the mobile application interface after displaying sentiment analysis results; 
 upon detecting selection of the share button, transmitting the journal entry text via SMS to the stored phone number; and 
 creating a sharing event record in the relational database comprising a timestamp and the sentiment score, wherein the sharing event record is accessible to the therapist. 



         9 . The method of  claim 2 , wherein analyzing sentiment further comprises:
 detecting neglect indicators comprising:
 absence language including “ignored,” “dismissed,” “unavailable,” or “silent”; 
 emotional void language including “empty,” “alone,” “invisible,” or “disconnected”; and 
 disengagement language including “didn&#39;t respond,” “walked away,” or “shut down”; 
   when neglect indicators are detected, assigning a sentiment score of zero; and generating a reasoning explanation highlighting the emotional disconnection described in the journal entry. For more complex entries, neglect may be detected along with a score below zero.   


         10 . The method of  claim 2 , wherein analyzing sentiment further comprises:
 detecting abuse indicators comprising:
 verbal attack language including “yelled,” “screamed,” “insulted,” or “cursed at”; 
 contempt language including “mocked,” “ridiculed,” “belittled,” or “degraded”; 
 control language including “demanded,” “forbade,” “controlled,” or “isolated”; 
 threat language including “threatened,” “intimidated,” or “menaced”; and 
 physical harm language including “pushed,” “grabbed,” “hit,” or “hurt”; 
   assigning severity scores ranging from −1 to −10 based on the intensity and frequency of detected abuse indicators; and   generating a reasoning explanation identifying the specific abusive behaviors detected and their classification on the abuse scale.   


         11 . The method of  claim 2 , further comprising:
 implementing privacy protection by:
 storing full journal entry text exclusively on the client mobile device in encrypted local storage; 
 transmitting only sentiment scores, timestamps, confidence scores, and metadata to the remote server; 
 configuring the relational database schema to exclude fields for storing journal entry text content; and 
 providing the therapist with access only to quantitative sentiment data and summary statistics, without access to the qualitative journal entry text unless the client explicitly shares the text during a therapy session. 



         12 . The computer-readable storage medium of  claim 3 , wherein the instructions further cause the processor to:
 implement repair attempt detection by:
 maintaining a repair lexicon comprising:
 apology patterns including “I&#39;m sorry,” “I apologize,” “forgive me,”or “my mistake”; 
 reconciliation patterns including “let&#39;s talk,” “can we work this out,”“I want to fix this,” or “how can I make it better”; 
 responsibility patterns including “I was wrong,” “my fault,” “I shouldn&#39;t have,” or “I understand why you&#39;re upset”; 
 affirmation patterns including “I love you,” “you matter,” “we&#39;ll get through this,” or “I value our relationship”; 
 classifying the journal entry as containing a repair attempt; 
 adding +2 to the preliminary sentiment score; and 
 annotating the reasoning explanation with identification of the specific repair language detected; 

   scanning the journal entry text for matches to patterns in the repair lexicon; when at least one match is detected:
 classifying the journal entry as containing a repair attempt; 
 adding +2 to the preliminary sentiment score; and 
 annotating the reasoning explanation with identification of the specific repair language detected. 



         13 . The computer-readable storage medium of  claim 3 , wherein the instructions further cause the processor to:
 implement contextual sentiment adjustment by:   detecting negation terms including “not,” “never,” “no,” “don&#39;t,” or “didn&#39;t” within a three-word window of sentiment terms;   when a negation term precedes a negative sentiment term, reversing the polarity by converting the negative value to a positive value;   when a negation term precedes a positive sentiment term, reversing the polarity by converting the positive value to a negative value;   detecting intensifier terms including “very,” “extremely,” “really,” “so,” or “completely” within a two-word window of sentiment terms;   when an intensifier term is detected, multiplying the associated sentiment value by a scaling factor ranging from 1.5 to 2.0; and   adjusting the preliminary sentiment score based on the polarity reversals and intensity amplifications. If the entry does not contain indications of care, neglect or abuse, it is considered neutral and not inserted into the database.   


         14 . The system of  claim 1 , further comprising:
 a client onboarding module configured to:
 present a tutorial interface to first-time users explaining the Care-Neglect-Abuse continuum model; 
 provide example journal entries with corresponding sentiment scores for educational purposes; 
 enable a practice mode wherein clients can enter non-recorded trial journal entries and receive sentiment analysis feedback without storing the entries in the database; and 
 facilitate therapist-guided initial entry creation by presenting prompts suggested by the therapist during an onboarding therapy session. 



         15 . The system of  claim 1 , wherein the therapist reporting interface further comprises:
 a comparative analysis module configured to:
 retrieve event records for both the first client and the second client in a client pair; 
 generate separate scatter plots for each client displaying their respective sentiment scores over time; 
 calculate correlation metrics measuring the degree to which sentiment patterns of the first client and second client align or diverge; 
 identify periods of sentiment divergence where one client&#39;s scores trend positively while the other&#39;s trend negatively; 
 highlight periods of sentiment convergence where both clients&#39; scores trend in similar directions, indicating shared emotional experiences; and 
 display a combined scatter plot and trend line for both client entries. 



         16 . The method of  claim 2 , further comprising:
 conducting validation research by:
 enrolling a first cohort of 3-5 trained therapists and their client pairs; 
 implementing manual sentiment scoring by subject matter experts (SMEs) who review journal entries and independently assign scores on the Care-Neglect-Abuse continuum; 
 comparing AI-generated sentiment scores to SME-assigned scores to calculate accuracy metrics; 
 identifying discrepancies where AI and SME scores differ by more than 2 points on the −10 to +10 scale; 
 refining the sentiment lexicon and natural language processing algorithms based on the identified discrepancies; 
 enrolling a second cohort of 30+ therapists and 300+ client pairs; 
 implementing parallel AI and therapist assessment wherein both automated sentiment analysis and therapist clinical judgment evaluate relationship progress; and 
 validating therapeutic effectiveness by measuring outcome metrics including relationship satisfaction scores, conflict resolution rates, and therapy retention rates. 



         17 . The computer-readable storage medium of  claim 3 , wherein the instructions further cause the processor to:
 calculate the confidence score by:
 assigning a base confidence value of 0.5; 
 increasing the confidence value by 0.1 for each strong lexicon match where a sentiment term appears in the sentiment lexicon with a clear, unambiguous definition; 
 decreasing the confidence value by 0.1 for each ambiguous term or phrase that could have multiple interpretations; 
 decreasing the confidence value by 0.2 when sarcasm indicators are detected, including:
 contradictions between literal word meaning and likely intended meaning; 
 exaggerated praise in contexts suggesting negative emotions; or 
 ironic punctuation patterns such as excessive exclamation marks; 

 increasing the confidence value by 0.1 when explicit emotion words are present, including “I felt,” “I was,” or “it made me feel”; 
 capping the confidence value at a maximum of 1.0 and a minimum of 0.0; and 
 transmitting a low-confidence alert to the therapist reporting interface when the confidence value falls below 0.6, indicating that the journal entry may benefit from human review. 



         18 . The system of  claim 1 , further comprising:
 an adaptation module configured to enable solo therapy mode by:
 modifying the client registration process to accept single client accounts not linked to a partner; 
 configuring the mobile application to present journal entry prompts focused on self-reflection rather than dyadic relationship interactions; 
 adapting the sentiment analysis engine to analyze personal growth indicators including:
 self-compassion language; 
 insight and awareness statements; 
 goal-setting and progress descriptions; and 
 emotional regulation strategies; 

 generating individual progress reports for the therapist showing trends in self-care, self-criticism, and emotional well-being over time; and 
 providing the solo client with access to personal sentiment trend visualizations for self-monitoring purposes. 



         19 . The system of  claim 1 , wherein the AI sentiment analysis engine is implemented using:
 a machine learning model trained on a dataset comprising:
 annotated relationship journal entries labeled by licensed therapists with sentiment scores on the Care-Neglect-Abuse continuum; 
 relationship therapy session transcripts with identified instances of care, neglect, abuse, and repair attempts; 
 validated psychological assessment instruments measuring relationship satisfaction, conflict patterns, and attachment styles; and 
   wherein the machine learning model comprises one or more of:
 a transformer-based natural language processing architecture; 
 a bidirectional encoder representations from transformers (BERT) model fine-tuned on relationship-specific text; 
 a long short-term memory (LSTM) recurrent neural network configured to capture temporal dependencies in journal narratives; or 
 an ensemble model combining outputs from multiple sentiment classification algorithms using weighted voting or stacking methods.

