What is claimed is: 

         1 . A patient interface comprising:
 a rigid frame defining a breathing chamber configured to receive pressurized respiratory gas; and   a thermoformable seal coupled to the frame and configured to form a sealing interface with a user during use;   
       wherein the thermoformable seal comprises cross-linked polycaprolactone, the cross-linked polycaprolactone being directly connected to the rigid frame and initially provided in a non-customized shape; 
       wherein a substantial portion of the facial-contacting portion of the thermoformable seal is positioned beyond the outer perimeter of the portion of the rigid frame forming the breathing chamber, in a cheek region or a nasal bridge region, as measured in the coronal plane; 
       wherein the thermoformable seal softens when heated to a temperature of about 50-70° C.; 
       wherein the cross-linked polycaprolactone is configured to retain at least part of the non-customized shape of the thermoformable seal when heated above the softening temperature; 
       wherein the cross-linked polycaprolactone is configured for direct contact with the patient&#39;s face during molding; 
       wherein the thermoformable seal is configured to be moldable against the patient&#39;s face while softened to conform to the patient&#39;s facial contours; 
       wherein the thermoformable seal retains at least part of a molded customized shape after cooling below the temperature range; 
       wherein the thermoformable seal is substantially rigid at temperatures below the softening temperature range; 
       and wherein the rigid frame remains substantially rigid at temperatures below 100° C. 


         2 . The patient interface as claimed in  claim 1 , wherein the thermoformable seal substantially returns toward the non-customized shape after being reheated above its softening temperature, has a hardness greater than 15 Shore D at temperatures between 10° C. and 30° C., and has a hardness below Shore A 10 when heated above its softening temperature. 


         3 . The patient interface as claimed in  claim 1 , wherein at least a portion of the facial-contacting portion of the thermoformable seal is substantially free of silicone or soft elastomer material. 


         4 . The patient interface as claimed in  claim 1 , wherein a substantial portion of the facial-contacting portion of the thermoformable seal is configured to form a sealing interface with the patient&#39;s face without curving inwardly toward the breathing chamber where the thermoformable seal contacts the patient&#39;s face. 


         5 . The patient interface as claimed in  claim 4 , wherein the thermoformable seal further comprises a silicone layer positioned over at least a portion of the thermoformable seal. 


         6 . The patient interface as claimed in  claim 1 , wherein the thermoformable seal extends beyond the outer perimeter of the rigid frame in at least a cheek region or a nasal bridge region and forms a seal with a high aspect ratio where the thermoformable seal contacts the patient&#39;s face, the high aspect ratio being between 6.67 and 20, the high aspect ratio defined as a ratio of seal extension beyond the rigid frame to seal thickness. 


         7 . The patient interface as claimed in  claim 1 , wherein the facial-contacting portion of the thermoformable seal is configured to distribute mask retention forces over an enlarged facial contact area to reduce localized facial pressure. 


         8 . A patient interface comprising:
 a rigid frame defining a breathing chamber configured to receive pressurized respiratory gas; and   a thermoformable seal coupled to the frame;   
       wherein the thermoformable seal comprises cross-linked polycaprolactone directly connected to the rigid frame and initially provided in a non-customized shape; 
