What is claimed as new and desired to be protected by Letters Patent of the United States is: 

         1 . A system for collecting a tennis ball on a first tennis court and
 for controllably releasing the ball toward a target, comprising:   a mobile retriever, comprising:
 a drive means; 
 a steering means; 
 a receiver; 
 an on-board power supply; 
 a camera; 
 a processor-controller with inputs for receiving control and data input signals including video data from the camera and outputs for executed programmed instructions including retriever command and control signals directing retriever locomotion for ball retrievals and releases to and from a retriever standby location, including inertial ball release horizontally along the first tennis court surface toward the target utilizing retriever momentum; 
 a ball collector section including an opening sized for receiving and releasing a ball therethrough; 
 a ball proximity sensor for sensing a ball in the ball collector; and 
 a detent operated by the controller-processor for selectively securing and releasing the ball; and 
   a remote control.   


         2 . The system of  claim 1 , further comprising a signal emitter positioned at the standby location for transmitting a signal to the receiver to guide the retriever to the standby location. 


         3 . The system of  claim 2 , further comprising a docking turntable. 


         4 . The system of  claim 1 , wherein the processor-controller further comprises a programmed grid including first tennis on-court ball retrieval areas and is programmed with instructions that upon execution direct the retriever to a user-selected ball retrieval area. 


         5 . The system of  claim 4 , wherein the grid further comprises off-court ball retrieval areas. 


         6 . The system of  claim 4 , wherein the retriever further comprises an on-board ball locator for locating a ball within the user-selected ball retrieval area to facilitate a ball retrieval. 


         7 . The system of  claim 6 , wherein the grid further comprises off-court ball retrieval areas. 


         8 . The system of  claim 7 , wherein the off-court ball retrieval areas include at least a second tennis court. 


         9 . The system of  claim 6 , wherein the retriever further comprises a vertical ball ejector and wherein the processor-controller is further programmed with instructions that upon execution direct the retriever to release the ball in a vertical direction toward a target. 


         10 . The system of  claim 6 , wherein the retriever further comprises a horizontal ball ejector and wherein the processor-controller is further programmed with instructions that upon execution direct the retriever to activate the horizontal ball ejector during a ball release in a horizontal direction toward a target. 


         11 . The system of  claim 1 , wherein the processor-controller further comprises a heuristic learning algorithm for identifying a player-preferred retrieval based on historical player-signaled retrieval information to initiate a retrieval without a player-initiated retrieval command. 


         12 . The system of  claim 11 , further comprising an automatic mode for initiating a retrieval. 


         13 . The system of  claim 12 , further comprising a retriever status indicator. 


         14 . A mobile retriever for collecting a tennis ball on a first tennis court and for controllably releasing the ball toward a target, comprising
 a drive means;   a steering means;   a receiver;   an on-board power supply;   a camera;   a processor-controller with inputs for receiving control and data input signals including video data from the camera and outputs for executed programmed instructions including retriever command and control signals directing retriever locomotion for ball retrievals and releases to and from a retriever standby location, including inertial ball release horizontally along the first tennis court surface toward the target utilizing retriever momentum;   a ball collector section including an opening sized for receiving and releasing a ball therethrough;   a ball proximity sensor for sensing a ball in the ball collector; and   a detent operated by the controller-processor for selectively securing and releasing the ball.   


         15 . The retriever of  claim 14 , wherein the processor-controller further comprises a programmed grid including first tennis court ball retrieval areas and is programmed with instructions that upon execution direct the retriever to a user-selected ball retrieval area to retrieve a ball. 


         16 . The retriever of  claim 15 , further comprising an on-board ball locator for locating a ball within the user-selected ball retrieval area to facilitate a ball retrieval. 


         17 . The retriever of  claim 15 , wherein the grid further comprises off-court ball retrieval areas. 


         18 . The retriever of  claim 17 , wherein the off-court ball retrieval areas include at least a second tennis court. 


         19 . The retriever of  claim 14 , wherein the processor-controller further comprises a heuristic learning algorithm for identifying a player-preferred retrieval based on historical player-signaled retrieval information to direct a retrieval without a player-initiated retrieval command. 


         20 . A method for retrieving a ball and for delivering a towel on a sports playing surface, comprising:
 providing a mobile retriever, comprising:
 a drive means; 
 a steering means; 
 a receiver; 
 an on-board power supply; 
 a processor-controller, having inputs for receiving control and data input signals and outputs for retriever command and control signals directing retriever locomotion, and including a heuristic learning algorithm for identifying a preferred retriever command according to prior player-initiated retriever commands and directing the retriever accordingly; 
 a ball collector section, including a tray for supporting a ball, and an opening sized for receiving and releasing a ball therethrough; 
 a ball proximity sensor for sensing a ball in the ball collector; 
 a detent operated by the controller-processor for selectively securing and releasing the ball; 
   positioning the retriever at a standby location proximate to the sports playing surface;   directing the retriever via processor-controller outputs to conduct a ball collection and a ball release toward the target; and   directing the retriever via processor-controller outputs to the standby location following a retrieval and ball delivery to the target.

