What is claimed is: 

         1 . A modular ozone-based disinfection system configured to selectively control an ozone-to-hydroxyl-radical treatment ratio, the modular ozone-based disinfection system comprising:
 a purification configuration comprising an air inlet, an air outlet, an air circulation subsystem, and an airflow path extending between the air inlet and the air outlet;   an electrolytic ozone generator fluidly connected to a water source and configured to generate ozone from water;   a hydroxyl radical catalyst module positioned along the airflow path and configured to receive ozone-containing air comprising the ozone generated by the electrolytic ozone generator;   a heater operatively associated with the hydroxyl radical catalyst module and configured to heat the ozone-containing air within the hydroxyl radical catalyst module; and   a controller operatively connected to the heater and configured to selectively operate the heater at a selected heater setting from among a plurality of heater settings;   
       wherein the selected heater setting controls, at least in part, a temperature to which the ozone-containing air is heated within the hydroxyl radical catalyst module, and 
       wherein the temperature controls, at least in part, a relative amount of ozone and hydroxyl radicals in a treated airflow discharged from the air outlet. 


         2 . The modular ozone-based disinfection system of  claim 1 , wherein the plurality of heater settings comprises a plurality of discrete heater settings corresponding to a plurality of respective ozone-to-hydroxyl-radical treatment ratios. 


         3 . The modular ozone-based disinfection system of  claim 2 , wherein a lower heater setting of the plurality of discrete heater settings produces an ozone-dominant treated airflow, and wherein a higher heater setting of the plurality of discrete heater settings produces a hydroxyl-radical-dominant treated airflow. 


         4 . The modular ozone-based disinfection system of  claim 3 , wherein the lower heater setting comprises a no-heat setting or a minimal-heat setting, and wherein the higher heater setting comprises a maximum-heat setting. 


         5 . The modular ozone-based disinfection system of  claim 2 , wherein the plurality of discrete heater settings comprises ten heater settings, and wherein intermediate heater settings between a first heater setting and a tenth heater setting produce graduated intermediate ozone-to-hydroxyl-radical treatment ratios. 


         6 . The modular ozone-based disinfection system of  claim 1 , further comprising a temperature sensor positioned to detect an operating temperature of the hydroxyl radical catalyst module or of the ozone-containing air, wherein the controller is configured to adjust operation of the heater based at least in part on feedback from the temperature sensor. 


         7 . A method of using the modular ozone-based disinfection system of  claim 1 , the method comprising the steps of:
 generating the ozone from the water using the electrolytic ozone generator;   directing the ozone-containing air along the airflow path and into the hydroxyl radical catalyst module;   selecting the selected heater setting from among the plurality of heater settings;   heating the ozone-containing air within the hydroxyl radical catalyst module to the temperature corresponding to the selected heater setting; and   discharging the treated airflow from the air outlet with the relative amount of ozone and hydroxyl radicals corresponding to the selected heater setting.   


         8 . A modular ozone-based disinfection system configured to selectively control an ozone-to-hydroxyl-radical treatment ratio, the modular ozone-based disinfection system comprising:
 a purification configuration comprising an air inlet, an air outlet, an air circulation subsystem, and an airflow path extending between the air inlet and the air outlet;   an electrolytic ozone generator configured to generate ozone from water and introduce the ozone into the airflow path to form ozone-containing air;   a hydroxyl radical catalyst module positioned along the airflow path and comprising a heating region configured to heat the ozone-containing air;   a hydroxyl augmentation and ozone reduction module positioned downstream of the hydroxyl radical catalyst module along the airflow path; and   a controller configured to execute a selected oxidant-output profile from among a plurality of oxidant-output profiles;   
       wherein each oxidant-output profile of the plurality of oxidant-output profiles is associated with a respective heater output level for the heating region; 
       wherein the selected oxidant-output profile causes the controller to select the respective heater output level associated with the corresponding ozone-to-hydroxyl-radical treatment ratio; and 
       wherein the respective heater output level controls conversion of the ozone into hydroxyl radicals to produce a corresponding ozone-to-hydroxyl-radical treatment ratio in a treated airflow discharged through the air outlet. 


         9 . The modular ozone-based disinfection system of  claim 8 , wherein the plurality of oxidant-output profiles comprises an ozone-rich profile, a mixed ozone-and-hydroxyl-radical profile, and a hydroxyl-radical-rich profile. 


         10 . The modular ozone-based disinfection system of  claim 9 , wherein the ozone-rich profile corresponds to a lower heater output level, wherein the hydroxyl-radical-rich profile corresponds to a higher heater output level, and wherein the mixed ozone-and-hydroxyl-radical profile corresponds to an intermediate heater output level between the lower heater output level and the higher heater output level. 


         11 . The modular ozone-based disinfection system of  claim 8 , further comprising a user interface operatively connected to the controller and configured to receive a user selection of the selected oxidant-output profile. 


         12 . The modular ozone-based disinfection system of  claim 8 , further comprising an ozone sensor positioned to detect an ozone concentration of the treated airflow, wherein the controller is configured to adjust the respective heater output level based at least in part on the ozone concentration. 


         13 . The modular ozone-based disinfection system of  claim 8 , further comprising a humidity sensor positioned to detect water vapor available in the ozone-containing air, wherein the controller is configured to adjust the respective heater output level based at least in part on the water vapor available in the ozone-containing air. 


         14 . A method of using the modular ozone-based disinfection system of  claim 8 , the method comprising the steps of: 
       selecting the selected oxidant-output profile from among the plurality of oxidant-output profiles; 
       generating the ozone from the water using the electrolytic ozone generator; 
       introducing the ozone into the airflow path to form the ozone-containing air; 
       heating the ozone-containing air in the heating region according to the respective heater output level associated with the selected oxidant-output profile; 
       passing the treated airflow through the hydroxyl augmentation and ozone reduction module; and 
       discharging the treated airflow through the air outlet with the corresponding ozone-to-hydroxyl-radical treatment ratio. 


         15 . A modular ozone-based disinfection system configured to selectively control an ozone-to-hydroxyl-radical treatment ratio, the modular ozone-based disinfection system comprising: 
       a purification configuration comprising an air inlet, an air outlet, an air circulation subsystem, and an airflow path extending between the air inlet and the air outlet; 
       a tank configuration configured to supply water to an electrolytic ozone generator; 
       the electrolytic ozone generator configured to generate ozone from the water supplied by the tank configuration; 
       a hydroxyl radical catalyst module positioned along the airflow path and configured to receive ozone-containing air comprising the ozone generated by the electrolytic ozone generator; 
       a heater operatively associated with the hydroxyl radical catalyst module; 
       a selector comprising a plurality of graduated oxidant output settings; and 
       a controller operatively connected to the selector and the heater, 
       wherein each graduated oxidant output setting of the plurality of graduated oxidant output settings corresponds to a respective heater setting from among a plurality of heater settings; 
       wherein the controller is configured to operate the heater at a heater temperature corresponding to a selected graduated oxidant output setting of the plurality of graduated oxidant output settings; and 
       wherein the heater temperature controls, at least in part, conversion of the ozone into hydroxyl radicals so that the treated airflow has an ozone-to-hydroxyl-radical treatment ratio corresponding to the selected graduated oxidant output setting. 


         16 . The modular ozone-based disinfection system of  claim 15 , wherein the tank configuration comprises a replaceable water cartridge configured to supply the water to the electrolytic ozone generator. 


         17 . The modular ozone-based disinfection system of  claim 15 , wherein the tank configuration is fluidly connected to a dehumidifier configuration configured to extract water from ambient air and deliver the water to the tank configuration. 


         18 . The modular ozone-based disinfection system of  claim 15 , wherein the tank configuration is fluidly connected to a source water configuration configured to receive the water from an external water supply. 


         19 . The modular ozone-based disinfection system of  claim 15 , wherein the purification configuration is configured for installation in a refrigerated or enclosed space, and wherein the controller is configured to select the selected graduated oxidant output setting based at least in part on a treatment mode for the refrigerated or enclosed space. 


         20 . A method of using the modular ozone-based disinfection system of  claim 15 , the method comprising the steps of: 
       supplying the water from the tank configuration to the electrolytic ozone generator; 
       generating the ozone from the water using the electrolytic ozone generator; 
       directing the ozone-containing air through the hydroxyl radical catalyst module; 
       selecting the selected graduated oxidant output setting from the plurality of graduated oxidant output settings; 
       operating the heater at the heater temperature corresponding to the selected graduated oxidant output setting; and 
       producing the treated airflow having the ozone-to-hydroxyl-radical treatment ratio corresponding to the selected graduated oxidant output setting.

What is claimed is: 

         1 . A system comprising: 
       1a. a platform host with at least one processor and memory in communication with user devices over a network, the platform host executing a workflow runtime that manages tasks with a milestone-based double approval mechanism and on-platform collaboration and communications; 
       1b. a data lake storing operational workflow events, milestone notes and deliverables, time and screen tracking data, communications content and metadata, programmatic payment confirmations, and provider demonstrations and execution logs captured within the workflow runtime; 
       1c. a tagging and enrichment pipeline that categorizes the stored data to a task and skill ontology to produce machine-learning-ready corpora and feature vectors with lineage entries; 
       1d. a provenance subsystem that records consent, lineage, and model and agent versioning for artifacts in the data lake and corpora, optionally anchored using blockchain-enabled features; 
       1e. a training orchestrator configured to train or fine-tune advanced AI models using the corpora, including models selected from foundational models and large language models; and 


         2 . The system of  claim 1 , wherein the training orchestrator supports supervised learning on historical outcomes, imitation learning from demonstrations, and feedback learning derived from milestone approvals and rejections logged by the double approval mechanism, and wherein the training orchestrator observes and learns from user completion and execution of tasks such that the advanced AI models are trained via execution data, supporting files, and demonstrations provided by the user, enabling the system to watch and learn from user behavior in real time. 


         3 . The system of  claim 1 , wherein the advanced AI models comprise large language models and foundational models obtained via a procurement interface and adapted to the ontology using platform-tagged corpora, and wherein the platform host functions as an expert system that directly feeds operational data and learned patterns into a proprietary advanced AI training infrastructure comprising the data lake, the tagging and enrichment pipeline, the provenance subsystem, the training orchestrator, and the agent factory, providing users with the advanced AI models and artificial general intelligence training, creation, ownership, and augmentation capabilities. 


         4 . The system of  claim 1 , wherein the provenance subsystem anchors lineage entries to a blockchain-backed append-only log that also supports tokenized programmatic payments. 


         5 . The system of  claim 1 , further comprising a model and agent registry storing versioned checkpoints, capability manifests, evaluation metrics on holdout tasks, and deployment entitlements linked to digital IDs. 


         6 . The system of  claim 1 , wherein the platform host integrates with one or more of an applicant tracking system, a vendor management system, a human resources management platform, an external sourcing API, or any additional internal or external tech stack or interface, and wherein the AI agents consume and contribute to those systems via standardized adapters mapped to the tagging ontology. 


         7 . The system of  claim 1 , wherein the training orchestrator and tagging pipeline support selective unlearning by identifying and removing features and training samples associated with rescinded consent via lineage traversal in the provenance subsystem. 


         8 . The system of  claim 1 , wherein sensitive enterprise task data received via secure APIs may optionally be processed transiently in volatile memory during feature computation, and wherein purging of data, derived corpora, model checkpoints, and AI agents from the platform host is not performed by default and instead occurs only upon deletion of, or a request to delete, the corresponding data or AI agent by an authorized data holder or agent owner. 


         9 . The system of  claim 1 , wherein the agent factory supports instantiation of replacement AI agents for particular tasks based on historical performance data and compatibility metrics when a provider is removed or unavailable, and wherein AI agents function as digital providers, digital agents, or digital employees that are assignable to tasks and projects in place of or alongside human providers, subject to the workflow management and double approval mechanisms. 


         10 . The system of  claim 1 , further comprising digital skilling functionality comprising a tech-enabled workspace in which users complete training, orientation, learning, development, and preparation tasks within an interactive environment that captures user interactions as demonstration data for training AI agents, and wherein the platform host constructs a digital resume aggregating verified skills, credentials, task history, and performance metrics for each user, and a digital ID consolidating verified identity, credentials, and entitlements for each user, derived from one or more data points or the data lake and validated against one or more external clearinghouses or service bureaus. 


         11 . The system of  claim 1 , wherein the training orchestrator is configured to train the advanced AI models to perform any type of skill or job as an advanced AI agent, achieving end-to-end execution of tasks including high-level and advanced tasks across all skill domains. 


         12 . The system of  claim 1 , wherein the platform host supports a distributed and privacy-preserving architecture wherein each platform and module maintains its own private data, machine learning models, foundational models, and large language models on designated client, talent, or mothership servers, with blockchain-enabled features offered as optional additions; wherein the platform host further comprises a network sharing interface through which users selectively share data and models with all on-network platforms or with designated on-network platforms; wherein sharing is recorded in the provenance subsystem and is revocable at any time with access termination propagated to affected network platforms; and wherein prior to sharing to each network the platform host generates a network contract for digital review and electronic signature by the user, with executed network contracts stored in the provenance subsystem. 


         13 . A computer-implemented method comprising: 
       13a. ingesting, at a platform host, workflow events, milestone approvals and rejections, notes and deliverables, communications, programmatic payment confirmations, and provider demonstrations collected during execution of a milestone-based double approval process; 
       13b. tagging the ingested data according to a task and skill ontology to form corpora with feature vectors and lineage metadata recorded in a provenance subsystem; 
       13c. training or fine-tuning at least one advanced AI model using the corpora to produce a task-scoped AI agent; 
       13d. packaging the trained model as the task-scoped AI agent with a capability manifest and deployment policies enforced by a policy engine integrated with the double approval mechanism; 
       13e. deploying the agent into the workflow runtime to co-pilot or execute tasks and to generate objective completion signals or approval recommendations; and 
       13f. metering and remitting programmatic payments for agent training and inference using one or more of traditional currency, tokenized, cryptocurrency-compatible, subscription-based, or software-as-a-service-based mechanisms. 


         14 . The method of  claim 13 , further comprising performing real-time incremental training updates to the AI agent using newly ingested and tagged workflow events and demonstrations with lineage recorded in the provenance subsystem. 


         15 . The method of  claim 13 , wherein deploying the agent includes restricting autonomous execution to subtasks having predetermined objective completion criteria and routing proposed subjective actions through the double approval mechanism. 


         16 . The method of  claim 13 , wherein ownership and access rights to the AI agent are granted to a client or provider associated with the demonstrations and execution data used for training, as recorded in the provenance subsystem. 


         17 . The method of  claim 13 , wherein the tagging ontology includes labels for task schemas, skill taxonomies, approval categories, and outcome types, enabling consistent feature extraction from heterogeneous workflow and communications data. 


         18 . A non-transitory computer-readable medium storing instructions that, when executed by one or more processors of a platform host, cause the platform host to: 
       18a. maintain a data lake of operational and demonstration data tagged to an ontology with lineage; 
       18b. train or fine-tune an advanced AI model to produce an AI agent; 
       18c. register the AI agent with a capability manifest; 
       18d. enforce deployment policies using a policy engine integrated with a double approval mechanism; 
       18e. interface the AI agent with workflow and communications tools to perform objective subtasks autonomously and to generate approval recommendations for subjective tasks; and 
       18f. meter and settle programmatic payments for training and inference usage using one or more of traditional currency, tokenized, cryptocurrency-compatible, subscription-based, or software-as-a-service-based mechanisms. 


         19 . The non-transitory computer-readable medium of  claim 18 , wherein the instructions further cause the platform host to retrieve consented context from the data lake according to the capability manifest to condition agent outputs during task execution. 


         20 . The non-transitory computer-readable medium of  claim 18 , wherein the instructions cause the platform host to evaluate the AI agent on holdout tasks and demonstrations and to block deployment if evaluation metrics or policy constraints are not met, recording outcomes in the registry and provenance subsystem.

